<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puntland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puntland is an autonomous state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, declared in 1998 by clan leaders after the collapse of the central government. Its capital is Garowe, and it includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-guardafui">Cape Guardafui</a> and the ports of Bosaso and Eyl. It remains part of the Federal Republic of Somalia, unlike neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, which claims full independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puntland.jpg" alt="Puntland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Puntland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Puntland occupies the north-eastern portion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, about 212,510 square kilometres or roughly one third of the country. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> to the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> to the north, the Guardafui Channel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> to the east, Galmudug State to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to the south-west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-guardafui">Cape Guardafui</a> forms the tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, and Ras Hafun is the easternmost point on the African continent. The Golis Mountains, Galgala and Al Madow are among its mountains.</p><p>The climate is semi-arid and warm, favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>, and no area receives more than 400 millimetres of rain a year. The name derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a> mentioned in ancient Egyptian sources, whose exact location is still unknown. Many studies place Punt in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, while others place it elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Majeerteen Sultanate was founded in the mid-18th century and rose to prominence the next century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boqor-osman-mahamuud">Boqor Osman Mahamuud</a>. Centred on Alula, it controlled much of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in the 19th and early 20th centuries and ruled the towns of Alula, Bargaal, Bosaso and Hafun. It traded widely and made treaties with foreign powers. A struggle between Boqor Osman and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid">Yusuf Ali Kenadid</a> nearly destroyed it; Kenadid was forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, then returned in the 1870s with Hadhrami musketeers and established the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> in 1878.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-civil-war">Somali Civil War</a> began in 1991, a constitutional conference was held in Garoowe in 1998 over three months. Political leaders, traditional elders, business people and civil society representatives took part, and the autonomous Puntland State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> was established to provide services and security and to facilitate trade. Abdullahi Yusuf Ahmed was its founding president. A new regional constitution was passed on 15 June 2009, and in January 2009 the short-lived quasi-state of Maakhir was incorporated into Puntland. Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sool">Sool</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaag">Sanaag</a> and Ayn provinces in the west has been disputed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Puntland is a federal member state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> with its capital at Garoowe, and Bosaso is its commercial capital. Its president and vice president are elected by a unicameral parliament. About 39 per cent of its people were estimated to be nomads in 2016, and the population is mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a> especially well represented and a number of Mehri residents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is the main religion.</p><p>The coastline is 1,600 kilometres long and rich in fish. Livestock, fishing, frankincense, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, gum arabic and trade are the main sources of income, and the key trading centres are Bosaso and Galkayo. Puntland also has its own security force and its own public broadcaster, Puntland TV and Radio, founded in April 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puntland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Puntland</b>, the autonomous region of Somalia founded in 1998, disputes the Sool and Sanaag regions, whose Dhulbahante and Warsangeli clans are divided in their loyalties, and Somaliland forces took the town of Las Anod in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The region is now part of <b>Puntland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/majeerteen-sultanate">Majeerteen Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The north became the Republic of Somaliland, and <b>Puntland</b> formed as an autonomous state in 1998.</blockquote><a href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a></li></ul></section>
